1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
Algebra says how to compute the answer; calculus says what the answer must satisfy.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
A tuple calculus expression is a set former: the tuples t such that the condition holds.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
The condition is a logical formula, so the question becomes truth, not order of steps.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
Same question, two notations: the algebra keeps the rows with A = 10 and B = 20.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
Three atoms only: membership, attribute against attribute, attribute against a constant.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
Free variables build the answer; bound variables are internal to the condition.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
t of A picks an attribute by name; a domain variable is bound by position instead.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
Formulae close under not, and, or, implies, and the two quantifiers.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
Exists scans the relation and stops at the first witness.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
For-all is not-exists-not: a search for a counterexample that never succeeds.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
A join is an exists over the second relation; "for all" is a for-all with an exists inside.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
Measured: for-all and not-exists-not agree on every tuple tested.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
The shared variable v is the join: second attribute of R, first attribute of S.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
The free variable u is the first attribute of R, so the answer is a projection of P.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
Computed on the instance: u takes the values 1 and 2, and nothing else.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
Option B matches; A, C and D each join on the wrong pair of columns.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
Domain calculus swaps tuples for values: the answer is an ordered tuple of values.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
Membership is positional: angle-bracket u, v in R means u is the first attribute.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
The same query in both calculi returns the same two values here.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
This is the notation of query-by-example, and the notation the 2026 paper used.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
A calculus expression ranges over every tuple the domain allows, so an infinite domain means an infinite answer.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
The domain of a formula is its constants plus every value in the relations it names.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
An expression is safe when every value in its answer comes from that domain.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
The test used here: add a value nobody mentions, and see whether the answer changes.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
Four expressions, one instance, two domains: only one answer moves.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
The unsafe one is the naked complement: every tuple not in the relation.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
The other three each bind the output value to a relation.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
Answer C, and the reason is the growth, not the syntax.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
A for-all over an empty set of courses is true, so it is satisfied by everyone.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
With no Biology course the naive expression returns every value the domain allows.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
One existential over the enrolment relation fixes it: the answer is then just students.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
GATE 2017 Set 1 Q41 asks exactly this; the published key is all three.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
Codd: the basic algebra, safe tuple calculus and safe domain calculus are one language.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
Same power means a translation exists in both directions.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
Reachability is the standard counterexample: fixed quantifier depth cannot follow an unbounded chain.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
Measured: depth two finds every path up to two hops and misses the rest.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Four steps: bound the output, count the quantifiers, check the polarity, test the domain.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
The counts: the domain to the power of the arity bounds every answer.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Three traps: vacuous truth, an unbound output, and a negated unbound atom.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
Safety is the price of saying what you want instead of how to get it.

